    "updated_at": "2025-12-10T15:52:27Z",
    "body": "> Always escape `&` is clear and easy to understand.\r\n\r\nThis is what I'd really like to address with _at least_ a recommendation in the HTML standard that `&` is best escaped where applicable.\r\n\r\n@dmsnell linked to the [HTML3 spec](https://www.w3.org/MarkUp/html3/HTMLandSGML.html). [HTML4 also makes a recommendation:](https://www.w3.org/TR/html401/charset.html#h-5.3.2)\r\n\r\n> Authors should use \"&amp;amp;\" (ASCII decimal 38) instead of \"&\" to avoid confusion with the beginning of a character reference (entity reference open delimiter).\r\n\r\nEscaping `&` is something we understand implicitly and it's apparent in functions like [PHP's `htmlspecialchars`](https://www.php.net/manual/es/function.htmlspecialchars.php) or [Python's `html.escape`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/html.html#html.escape). \r\n\r\nAn explicit recommendation in the standard about `&` escaping would be a service to web developers.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
